t through palpation\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The dataset states that the digital rectal exam is also called a prostate exam. This indicates that, within the community, DRE is explicitly positioned as a method for direct physical examination of the prostate via the rectum. [Data: Entities (3229, 3215); Relationships (6381)]\\n\\nIn male patients, DRE is described as being used to evaluate the prostate’s size and texture and may help detect areas suspicious for tumor, often interpreted alongside PSA testing and other clinical risk factors. While PSA testing and broader risk factors are mentioned in narrative form, the concrete linked assessment targets in this community are SIZE and TEXTURE as exam-evaluated attributes. [Data: Entities (3215, 3237, 3238); Relationships (6384, 6385)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Key exam outputs include size/texture assessment and detection of irregular or hardened areas\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community identifies SIZE and TEXTURE as physical attributes of the prostate assessed during a DRE, with relationships describing DRE as a direct way to check these attributes. This frames the exam’s primary “outputs” as tactile assessments rather than numeric measurements. [Data: Entities (3237, 3238); Relationships (6384, 6385)]\\n\\nIRREGULAR OR HARDENED AREA is included as a notable abnormal finding that may be felt on the prostate during DRE and may indicate a tumor; the relationship explicitly states that an irregular or hardened part of the prostate felt during DRE is described as a possible sign of a tumor. This is the dataset’s most direct linkage between a palpation finding and a concerning clinical implication. [Data: Entities (3239); Relationships (6386)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"DRE is connected to structured follow-up after chemoradiation and longer-term surveillance\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A dedicated entity, FOLLOW-UP EXAM WITH DRE, describes a post-treatment evaluation using DRE performe